  "categories_confirmation": {
    "statement": "NO crate carries `categories`. Not one, anywhere.",
    "evidence_1": "`grep -rn '^categories' Cargo.toml src/rust/crates/*/Cargo.toml` -> rc=1, zero matches (workspace manifest and ALL NINE members, including the two publish=false ones).",
    "evidence_2": "The tomllib pass above independently asserts `package.categories is None` for each of the 7 publishable crates.",
    "evidence_3": "No `categories` key was added under `[workspace.package]` either, so nothing can arrive by inheritance.",
    "rationale_recorded_in_code": "The full seq-198 rationale (server-side-only slug validation, mid-tier, after immutable uploads; re-entered at 0.8.21) is written into src/rust/crates/fathomdb/Cargo.toml next to the keywords, and the other six manifests point at it. So a future contributor adding categories has to read why not first."
  },

  "readme_key_control_run": {
    "purpose": "The brief only required the `readme` key. I probed whether declaring it explicitly buys anything over cargo's auto-detection of a package-root README.md — i.e. whether the key is load-bearing or decorative.",
    "break": "src/rust/crates/fathomdb-query/Cargo.toml: readme = \"README.md\" -> readme = \"READ-ME-NOT-THERE.md\"",
    "command": "cargo package --no-verify --allow-dirty -p fathomdb-query",
    "rc": 101,
    "output": "error: readme `READ-ME-NOT-THERE.md` does not appear to exist (relative to .../fathomdb-query). Please update the readme setting in the manifest at .../Cargo.toml",
    "reading": "The explicit key IS load-bearing: it converts 'README silently missing from the crates.io page' (the 0.8.9 state on all 7 crates, which auto-detection produced without a murmur) into a LOCAL hard failure. A rename or a move now fails on the machine. This is the one piece of registry metadata in this leg that cargo really does validate locally.",
    "restore": "git checkout -- src/rust/crates/fathomdb-query/Cargo.toml -> `git status --porcelain` on that path is empty (byte-identical restore verified)."
  },

  "brief_and_design_citations_found_to_be_wrong": [
    {
      "id": "WRONG-1",
      "severity": "MATERIAL — it is the stated premise of HITL seq-198",
      "claim": "Brief Task C: 'They are validated LOCALLY by `cargo package`, so a mistake fails before anything leaves the machine — which is exactly why keywords are in scope and `categories` is not.' The design of record §4 says the same thing ('validated locally by `cargo package`, so a mistake fails before anything leaves the machine').",
      "finding": "FALSE for cargo 1.95.0. `cargo package` performs NO keyword validation whatsoever. Three deliberate controls on fathomdb-query, each run as a real `cargo package --no-verify --allow-dirty` (not just --list):",
      "controls": [
        "6 keywords (limit is 5)                     -> rc=0, 'Packaged 8 files'. Also rc=0 under `cargo package --list`.",
        "a 26-character keyword (limit is 20)        -> rc=0, 'Packaged 8 files'",
        "a keyword with a space and a '!' (illegal)  -> rc=0, 'Packaged 8 files'"
      ],
      "restore": "git checkout -- src/rust/crates/fathomdb-query/Cargo.toml after each; final `git status --porcelain` on that path empty.",
      "consequence": "Keywords are validated SERVER-SIDE by crates.io at publish, in the same place and at the same moment as categories. The seq-198 distinction between them therefore does NOT hold on the axis the ruling names. It survives on a DIFFERENT axis, and only that one: keyword rules are simple, closed and mechanically checkable offline (<=5, <=20 chars, [A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_+-]*), whereas a category must match an opaque server-side taxonomy that cannot be checked without the registry. So the residual risk here is much lower than for categories — but it is not zero for the reason given, and nothing in this repo currently checks it. A bad keyword on tier T5 would wedge the publish AFTER T1-T4 uploaded immutably: precisely the v0.8.9 partial-publish shape.",
      "what_I_did_about_it": "I did NOT rely on cargo. I wrote and ran an independent tomllib+regex check over all 7 committed manifests asserting count/length/charset/first-char, plus readme and categories-absence. All 7 clean. Evidence under keywords_per_crate.independent_mechanical_validation.",
      "HAND_OFF": "Slice 40 (R-20-PUB) should add this ~15-line assertion to the release preflight — the natural home is `scripts/verify-release-gates.sh` beside check 5, which already walks every publishable manifest for description/license/repository. I deliberately did NOT add it myself: that file is release-gate substrate and Slice 40's territory, and a concurrent leg could be in it. ALSO WORTH AN HITL LOOK: seq-198's stated premise is wrong, so the ruling deserves re-confirmation on its true basis (mechanically-checkable vs opaque-taxonomy) rather than on 'cargo validates one and not the other'."
    },
    {
      "id": "WRONG-2",
      "severity": "minor, but it contradicts Leg 1a's closure record",
      "claim": "Leg 1a's output.json: 'No warning emitted for carrying both `license` and `license-file`.'",
      "finding": "cargo 1.95.0 DOES warn, on every build/check/clippy/package/maturin invocation, once per crate: `warning: <crate>/Cargo.toml: only one of `license` or `license-file` is necessary. `license` should be used if the package license can be expressed with a standard SPDX expression.` Observed on `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` (rc still 0) and in the maturin build log.",
      "reading": "Cosmetic, NOT a defect, and NOT mine to fix. Leg 1a's mechanism requires `license-file` (it is what physically copies the repo-root LICENSE into each .crate) and the SPDX `license` field is what crates.io displays, so BOTH are wanted. The warning is cargo being unaware of that use. Flagged only so nobody reads the warning as new breakage introduced by this leg, and so the record is accurate."
    },
    {
      "id": "WRONG-3",
      "severity": "informational — the brief's numbers were right this time",
      "claim": "Brief: 'Leg 1a measured 14 files for the release-shaped build; you should see 15 with a README.'",
      "finding": "CONFIRMED EXACTLY. 14 before, 15 after, the delta being README.md. Recorded because the two prior legs both found the brief's file counts wrong; this one is right."
    },
    {
      "id": "WRONG-4",
      "severity": "informational",
      "claim": "Brief: 'agent-test.sh aborts at test-check-governed-surface-pin (~line 51), NOT at line 123.'",
      "finding": "CONFIRMED. `AGENT_VERBOSE=1 bash scripts/agent-test.sh` -> 7 suites ok, then `FAIL test-check-governed-surface-pin (exit=1, 3999ms)` and the aggregate stops. Aggregate rc=1 and is VACUOUS (TC-16); not quoted as a pass anywhere in this record."
    },
    {
      "id": "WRONG-5",
      "severity": "informational — a real trap for the next agent",
      "claim": "Brief: 'crates: `cargo package --list -p <crate>` for each of the 7 -> the README must appear in the list.'",
      "finding": "TRUE, but only at a CLEAN tree. At a dirty tree `cargo package --list` exits 101 and prints the DIRTY-FILE list instead of the package list — and that dirty-file list contains lines ending in `README.md`. A grep for README against that output reads as a pass while rc is 101. I hit this before committing. Capturing rc on the very next line is what caught it; the brief's own instruction saved the measurement."
    }
  ],

